WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.000
&gt;&gt; So, hi everybody.

00:00:06.001 --> 00:00:07.830
I'm going to present

00:00:07.831 --> 00:00:10.335
our conversation in
a breakout session with

00:00:10.336 --> 00:00:15.320
Don Bosco Youth at Risk
and Homelink Network NGO,

00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:17.940
which basically work at

00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:21.225
the forefront of finding
missing children in India.

00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:24.165
As was presented yesterday,

00:00:24.166 --> 00:00:26.440
missing children
are a big problem,

00:00:26.441 --> 00:00:27.780
that there's a lot of runaway and

00:00:27.781 --> 00:00:29.670
lost children on the street.

00:00:29.671 --> 00:00:32.540
Don Bosco and Homelink
are at the forefront

00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:35.310
of the fight of
trying to help them.

00:00:35.311 --> 00:00:38.670
In particular, just the

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.840
Don Bosco organization
by themselves,

00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:42.560
over the last 10 years,

00:00:42.561 --> 00:00:47.850
have found almost 300,000
children on the streets,

00:00:47.851 --> 00:00:50.460
and tried to help them by
finding their parents,

00:00:50.461 --> 00:00:53.270
and if they don't manage
to find their parents,

00:00:53.271 --> 00:00:55.760
and if they're placed
with them then actually

00:00:55.761 --> 00:00:59.060
take taking care of
them in the long term.

00:00:59.061 --> 00:01:03.290
So, out of those almost
300k missing children,

00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:05.810
they have actually
successfully managed to find

00:01:05.811 --> 00:01:10.415
the parents of 170,000 children,

00:01:10.416 --> 00:01:14.480
which is a huge impact in
basically reuniting these kids

00:01:14.481 --> 00:01:18.935
with their parents and
having a normal life.

00:01:18.936 --> 00:01:22.230
So, how do they do their work?

00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:26.025
Essentially, Homelink
has this front liners,

00:01:26.026 --> 00:01:28.860
which are people going
to the train stations,

00:01:28.861 --> 00:01:30.650
the bus stations, the markets,

00:01:30.651 --> 00:01:32.990
likely locations where
children can get

00:01:32.991 --> 00:01:36.124
lost or hangout if
they are runaways,

00:01:36.125 --> 00:01:37.390
and they're looking out

00:01:37.391 --> 00:01:39.375
essentially for kids
that look like they

00:01:39.376 --> 00:01:40.910
don't have an adult with

00:01:40.911 --> 00:01:42.800
them and approach them
and talk to them,

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:45.005
trying to identify
whether they need help.

00:01:45.006 --> 00:01:47.390
Once they encounter a lost child,

00:01:47.391 --> 00:01:48.620
they interview them in

00:01:48.621 --> 00:01:50.090
the beginning because
of moderation,

00:01:50.091 --> 00:01:52.160
they might give
very limited information,

00:01:52.161 --> 00:01:54.320
and these primary initial
information is

00:01:54.321 --> 00:01:57.080
related to social
workers that are in

00:01:57.081 --> 00:01:59.420
the office are trying then to use

00:01:59.421 --> 00:02:03.040
that limited key information
to quickly find the parents.

00:02:03.041 --> 00:02:04.560
With that information,
essentially,

00:02:04.561 --> 00:02:06.675
what they do is they
do Google searches

00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:09.400
about like school names,

00:02:09.401 --> 00:02:12.210
other landmarks that
they might mention,

00:02:12.211 --> 00:02:14.870
or a village name, etc.

00:02:14.871 --> 00:02:16.945
Then from there, if
they find anything

00:02:16.946 --> 00:02:19.320
called the Local
Police Department,

00:02:19.321 --> 00:02:22.300
try to see whether there's
a missing person report, etc.

00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:23.960
So, it's a very
time intensive and

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:25.910
very kind of detective work,

00:02:25.911 --> 00:02:29.790
so to say, with very extremely
limited information.

00:02:29.791 --> 00:02:32.595
So, in particular,

00:02:32.596 --> 00:02:35.840
one important thing is
basically being able to

00:02:35.841 --> 00:02:38.660
match a kid back to
their parents if their loss is

00:02:38.661 --> 00:02:40.310
extremely important
for their quality

00:02:40.311 --> 00:02:42.325
of life and long-term effects.

00:02:42.326 --> 00:02:44.630
Essentially, also even
the time that it

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:47.110
takes to match them
is also important.

00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:49.835
So the longer time that
they spend in the system,

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:53.040
the bigger impact that has

00:02:53.041 --> 00:02:55.160
like psychological
and emotionally

00:02:55.161 --> 00:02:57.030
on the child in the long run.

00:02:57.031 --> 00:03:00.030
So, our goal is can we
help our Don Bosco and

00:03:00.031 --> 00:03:02.480
Homelink to maximize
the matches and

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:05.615
minimize the time in which
they manage to do that.

00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:09.500
So, we identified
three particular areas

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:10.760
in which AI can help.

00:03:10.761 --> 00:03:13.940
The first one was using
historic data in order to

00:03:13.941 --> 00:03:17.710
identify key child information
that facilitates matches.

00:03:17.711 --> 00:03:20.480
So, as they have a lot
of historic data,

00:03:20.481 --> 00:03:22.880
what combinations
of key pieces of

00:03:22.881 --> 00:03:26.090
information increase
the likelihood of a match?

00:03:26.091 --> 00:03:29.180
The second one is can we use
historic data in order to

00:03:29.181 --> 00:03:32.060
predict hotspots
in space and time,

00:03:32.061 --> 00:03:33.470
so that they can allocate more

00:03:33.471 --> 00:03:34.940
of those front liners to be at

00:03:34.941 --> 00:03:36.380
the right places given

00:03:36.381 --> 00:03:38.710
the very limited resources
that they have.

00:03:38.711 --> 00:03:42.000
Finally, a more
long-term suggestion

00:03:42.001 --> 00:03:43.085
is how can we create

00:03:43.086 --> 00:03:45.120
a Missing Child Complaints
submission system?

00:03:45.121 --> 00:03:48.135
Right now, there is really
no unified system like that.

00:03:48.136 --> 00:03:50.435
It was mentioned, a lot
of people don't feel

00:03:50.436 --> 00:03:52.970
comfortable going to the police
and even if they do,

00:03:52.971 --> 00:03:54.290
the police don't
have a system that's

00:03:54.291 --> 00:03:56.150
accessible by the NGOs.

00:03:56.151 --> 00:03:58.550
So, can we create something

00:03:58.551 --> 00:04:00.920
for the parents to report
their missing children that's

00:04:00.921 --> 00:04:03.530
accessible and inclusive and

00:04:03.531 --> 00:04:07.590
most people will feel
comfortable using.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.075
So, on the first point.

00:04:11.076 --> 00:04:13.790
So, again, here using
the historic data, as we said,

00:04:13.791 --> 00:04:18.905
is almost 300k kids that were

00:04:18.906 --> 00:04:21.725
actually found for which
they have this process of

00:04:21.726 --> 00:04:23.180
the information that
they got whether

00:04:23.181 --> 00:04:24.845
they managed to match
the parents or not.

00:04:24.846 --> 00:04:26.720
We want to essentially build

00:04:26.721 --> 00:04:29.060
a predictive model that
tells us which subsets,

00:04:29.061 --> 00:04:33.000
which combinations of
features like age,

00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:36.760
clothing, maybe village name,
or school name,

00:04:36.761 --> 00:04:39.705
which of those things together
were the most effective

00:04:39.706 --> 00:04:43.625
in creating a successful search.

00:04:43.626 --> 00:04:45.815
So, as they're doing this,

00:04:45.816 --> 00:04:47.540
they're actually interviewing
the kids over time.

00:04:47.541 --> 00:04:50.360
So, there's even like
the sequential effect

00:04:50.361 --> 00:04:53.180
where they will ask
more questions.

00:04:53.181 --> 00:04:54.760
So, with this predictive model,

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:56.840
what we can do is we can use it

00:04:56.841 --> 00:04:59.480
to give guideline
to the interviewer.

00:04:59.481 --> 00:05:02.755
So, what questions to
ask like if they got,

00:05:02.756 --> 00:05:04.605
for example, the village name,

00:05:04.606 --> 00:05:08.330
what the other extra question
should they insist

00:05:08.331 --> 00:05:12.745
on in order to most effectively
triangulate the parents.

00:05:12.746 --> 00:05:16.820
So, maybe village name and xyz

00:05:16.821 --> 00:05:19.130
together is much
more effective than

00:05:19.131 --> 00:05:22.900
village name and other things
that they might ask.

00:05:22.901 --> 00:05:25.545
So, the second one are also,

00:05:25.546 --> 00:05:26.930
as I already mentioned,

00:05:26.931 --> 00:05:28.725
there's very limited resources.

00:05:28.726 --> 00:05:31.025
There's actually
a lot of variation

00:05:31.026 --> 00:05:33.320
in the number of missing children

00:05:33.321 --> 00:05:35.420
that are likely to be
at any particular time

00:05:35.421 --> 00:05:37.930
in any particular place in time.

00:05:37.931 --> 00:05:40.335
For example, there's
a seasonal variations,

00:05:40.336 --> 00:05:42.680
there's holidays that intensify

00:05:42.681 --> 00:05:44.390
the number of lost children,

00:05:44.391 --> 00:05:46.010
and there's the local festivals

00:05:46.011 --> 00:05:48.190
for related to particular places.

00:05:48.191 --> 00:05:49.490
So, can we just mind

00:05:49.491 --> 00:05:53.690
this rich historic
dataset to map where

00:05:53.691 --> 00:05:56.285
these hotspots are and
help them allocate

00:05:56.286 --> 00:05:59.605
strategically
their resource occasion.

00:05:59.606 --> 00:06:01.130
We believe that this
is something that is

00:06:01.131 --> 00:06:02.930
very quite easy to do.

00:06:02.931 --> 00:06:08.620
So, that would be our first
data exploration exploiting

00:06:08.621 --> 00:06:14.330
the readily available
and low hanging fruit

00:06:14.331 --> 00:06:15.980
in a blank and blank AI.

00:06:15.981 --> 00:06:18.620
Then, the one that I mentioned
before is going to be

00:06:18.621 --> 00:06:22.490
our second thing that we'll try.

00:06:22.491 --> 00:06:23.775
In the longer term,

00:06:23.776 --> 00:06:25.070
we're going to try to create

00:06:25.071 --> 00:06:27.745
this Missing Child Complaints
submission system that,

00:06:27.746 --> 00:06:30.020
again, has to be
accessible and inclusive,

00:06:30.021 --> 00:06:34.190
that many parents who have
low literacy and that are not

00:06:34.191 --> 00:06:35.870
using the Internet to go and

00:06:35.871 --> 00:06:39.000
create an online profile
for their kids.

00:06:39.001 --> 00:06:41.485
There's also
many languages in India.

00:06:41.486 --> 00:06:43.120
So, from that perspective,

00:06:43.121 --> 00:06:44.540
we came up with two things.

00:06:44.541 --> 00:06:46.760
First of all, there should be

00:06:46.761 --> 00:06:49.730
some way of them just
leaving a voice message,

00:06:49.731 --> 00:06:51.500
and in that case we can use AI to

00:06:51.501 --> 00:06:54.185
translate whatever local
language they're using to

00:06:54.186 --> 00:06:56.810
English and transcribe
it and then pass it into

00:06:56.811 --> 00:06:58.220
an actual child record it is

00:06:58.221 --> 00:07:02.035
searchable when a child is found.

00:07:02.036 --> 00:07:05.340
Additionally, if
the parents are able,

00:07:05.341 --> 00:07:08.150
they can upload an image

00:07:08.151 --> 00:07:10.635
or send a text message with
an image of their child,

00:07:10.636 --> 00:07:14.015
and then we can use AI
to match the parent

00:07:14.016 --> 00:07:16.140
submitted photos to photos of

00:07:16.141 --> 00:07:19.125
the children found by the NGO.

00:07:19.126 --> 00:07:21.520
As I mentioned, there's
already a lot of

00:07:21.521 --> 00:07:25.345
additional available data
over 10 years,

00:07:25.346 --> 00:07:28.800
almost 300,000 missing children

00:07:28.801 --> 00:07:31.870
found by Don Bosco and Homelink.

00:07:31.871 --> 00:07:36.550
They already have the data
with the time the others was

00:07:36.551 --> 00:07:38.980
found and the key pieces
of information

00:07:38.981 --> 00:07:41.575
that were gathered for
each child like gender,

00:07:41.576 --> 00:07:45.170
age, clothing details, etc.

00:07:45.171 --> 00:07:50.435
So, we're going to use this data
in our first two tasks.

00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:53.080
Some of the ethical concerns in

00:07:53.081 --> 00:07:55.390
the broader scope of
this operation that we

00:07:55.391 --> 00:07:58.360
identified is if we're
trying to create some kind

00:07:58.361 --> 00:08:01.955
of a system that works
across institutions,

00:08:01.956 --> 00:08:04.570
how can we resolve into
institutional issues and

00:08:04.571 --> 00:08:07.160
standardizations of
sharing the data in

00:08:07.161 --> 00:08:10.640
order to be able to
facilitate this matching with

00:08:10.641 --> 00:08:12.530
all of the data that's
available across

00:08:12.531 --> 00:08:15.205
all the stakeholders and
players in this field?

00:08:15.206 --> 00:08:19.265
There was some discussion
about using biometric data,

00:08:19.266 --> 00:08:20.990
so with respect to
that we have to

00:08:20.991 --> 00:08:23.925
worry about law compliance.

00:08:23.926 --> 00:08:26.570
Finally, we want to make
sure that whatever we do,

00:08:26.571 --> 00:08:30.055
we want to secure the data
from misuse by traffickers.

00:08:30.056 --> 00:08:33.330
So, thank you.

